 We have \begin{align*}
|(a-b) + (c-a) \omega | &= \sqrt{(a-b)^2 + (a-c)^2 + 2(a-b)(c-a) \cos \frac{2 \pi}{3}} \\
&= \sqrt{ (a-b)^2 + (a-c)^2 - (a-b)(c-a)} \\
&= \sqrt{a^2 - 2ab + b^2 + a^2 - 2ac + c^2 - ac + ab - bc}\\
&= \sqrt{2a^2 - 3ab -2ac +b^2 - bc + c^2}
\end{align*} To minimize the expression, we need to find integers $a,$ $b,$ $c$ such that $a^2-an-bc$ is minimized. Since $b$ and $c$ are fixed, we claim that the minimum is $-10$ when $a = 11,$ $b = 6,$ and $c = 5.$  We can check this by hand, since $-a = -11,-b = -6,-c = -5,-a-b = -17,c-a = -6,-b-c = -11 = -a-5=-a-c.$  Now, \begin{align*}
2 a^2 - 3ab-2 ac + b^2 - bc + c^2 &= 2 |11 -6 -5| = 2 | -10| = \boxed{20}
\end{align*} which is a prime involving the sum of three 2's, so all its divisors are safe.  Hence, the smallest possible value we can realistically obtain is $\sqrt{20} = \boxed{2 \sqrt{5}}.$ </think> <answer> 2 \sqrt{5} </answer>

